  "query_preview": "..\n(Thursday through Monday)\nIssued at 120 PM MDT Tue Sep 1 2026\n\nThe mid-level high pressure system aloft will start out centered\nover North Carolina Thursday, then migrate westward to NM's\neastern border with TX by Saturday, before drifting a little\nfarther east on Monday. Meanwhile, a low pressure system will\nremain stalled over the Pacific northwest coast until Monday, when\nit will begin to drift inland. Models depict this setup drawing\nanother surge of monsoon moisture northward over AZ and western NM\nstarting over southwest parts of the forecast area Thursday, then\nover more of central and western NM with increased thunderstorm\ncoverage and rainfall intensity Friday and Saturday. After PWATs\nbottom out over northwest areas around 0.50-0.75\" on Wednesday, \nthey will climb around 1.10-1.40\" across the forecast area by \nFriday, then remain elevated through Monday with a risk of locally\nheavy rainfall each afternoon mainly west of the Rio Grande, and \nat times over the central mountain chain as well. Some days will\nbe more convectively active than others, depending on when\ndisturbances embedded in the monsoon flow track northward over the\nforecast area. At this time, the GFS, which often does a good job\nforecasting such disturbances, indicates disturbances will cross \nwestern NM both Friday and Sunday. We'll monitor the timing of\nthese disturbances with future model runs. Also, the movement of \ncutoff upper level low pressure systems like the one over the \nPacific northwest are notoriously difficult to predict. So, the \nstrength of the monsoon moisture plume over the forecast area \nearly next week could be weaker or stronger than currently \nforecast depending on if or when the upper low actually shifts \ninland over the the northwest US. \n\nAfter continued warming Thursday, the increased monsoon moisture \nshould cause high temperatures to trend downward at the end of \nthe week, and potentially into early next week as well.",
